In an 
\begin_inset Formula $M/G/1$
\end_inset 

 queue inter-arrival times are exponentially distributed and service times
 are `generally' distributed.
 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Qupart

Use the concept of residual service time to show that, assuming customers
 are served in the order that they arrive, the average waiting time is given
 by
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\bar{R}}{1-\rho },\]

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{R}$
\end_inset 

 is the average residual service time and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho $
\end_inset 

 is the queue utilization factor.
 Use diagrams, where necessary, to explain your reasoning and state your
 assumptions clearly.
\begin_deeper 
\layout Mypercent

50
\end_deeper 
\layout Standard

\SpecialChar ~

\layout Qupart

Use a graphical argument to derive a formula for the residual service time,
 then combine this equation with the formula for the average waiting time
 above to show that
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{W}=\frac{\lambda \left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle }{2\left(1-\rho \right)},\]

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda $
\end_inset 

 is the arrival rate and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle X^{2}\right\rangle $
\end_inset 

 is the expectation of the squared service time.
